53:{"timestamp":"2026-07-29T10:08:56.388Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"The prompt is the “first longest string, or `None` for empty input” task. I’m choosing a direct scan with a strict length comparison: initializing from the first element makes tie behavior explicit, while the empty-list branch handles `None`. This keeps all program-defined work under the fixed semantics and gives the proof an inductive, unbounded list domain rather than bounded examples.","phase":"commentary","memory_citation":null}}
55:{"timestamp":"2026-07-29T10:08:59.735Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_041a8ed1ae91741f016a69d1385f3c8194af5b53d8e19f968d","name":"exec_command","arguments":"{\"cmd\":\"wc -l /codex-home/skills/shared/k-claims.md /codex-home/skills/shared/k-syntax.md && sed -n '1,320p' /codex-home/skills/shared/k-claims.md && sed -n '1,280p' /codex-home/skills/shared/k-syntax.md && sed -n '1,180p' reference-semantics/semantics/subscript.k && sed -n '1,120p' reference-semantics/semantics/int.k\",\"workdir\":\"/workspace\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":50000}","call_id":"call_xrlXDiL0n6RZAvmu3F2ZSgVR","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fad56-2d3e-7530-a30d-8104258d3287"}}}
56:{"timestamp":"2026-07-29T10:08:59.797Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"call_xrlXDiL0n6RZAvmu3F2ZSgVR","output":"Chunk ID: 54a429\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 2946\nOutput:\n  69 /codex-home/skills/shared/k-claims.md\n  65 /codex-home/skills/shared/k-syntax.md\n 134 total\n# K functions, claims, and proof modules\n\nUse this reference while writing `verification.k` helpers or `spec.k`\nreachability claims.\n\n## Functions and simplification\n\nDeclare deterministic helpers with `[function]`. Add `[total]` only when the\nequations cover the complete input domain:\n\n```k\nsyntax Int ::= sumTo(Int) [function, total]\nrule sumTo(N) => (N +Int 1) *Int N /Int 2 requires N >=Int 0\nrule sumTo(N) => 0                         requires N  <Int 0\n```\n\n### Attributes control rewriting, not truth\n\n`[function]`, `[total]`, `[simplification]`, `[concrete]`, priorities, and\nevaluator-related attributes change how K treats declarations and rules. These\nattributes do not prove coverage, consistency, or soundness. Establish those\nobligations under the\n[proof-extension soundness contract](proof-extension-soundness.md) before\nusing the rules in a proof.\n\nIn particular, check total functions for complete guarded coverage and check\nequations for overlap. A simplification rule is a trusted equation wherever its\nguard applies; prover success does not establish that equation's truth.\n\nUse `[simplification]` rules for algebraic facts the symbolic simplifier must\napply. Keep ordinary helper rules and functions in the kompiled definition.\nA proof module may contain claims and simplification rules, but not ordinary\nrules:\n\n```text\n[Error] Compiler: Only claims and simplification rules are allowed in proof modules.\n```\n\n## Reachability claims\n\nA claim rewrites a starting configuration to a destination configuration under\nits precondition:\n\n```k\nclaim [loop-inv]:\n      <k> while (n > 0) { s = s + n ; n = n - 1 ; } => .K ...</k>\n      <state> n |-> (N:Int => 0)\n              s |-> (S:Int => S +Int sumTo(N)) </state>\n  requires N >=Int 0\n```\n\n- `requires` constrains the starting configuration.\n- `ensures` constrains the destination when the cell rewrite alone does not\n  express the postcondition.\n- A variable introduced only on the right-hand side is existential and uses\n  the `?X` form.\n- `=> .K` requires the computation to be consumed on every covered path.\n\n## Claim labels\n\nThe command-line spelling depends on the declaration:\n\n| Declaration | CLI spelling |\n|---|---|\n| `claim [loop-inv]:` in module `SPEC` | `SPEC.loop-inv` |\n| `claim [label(loop-inv)]:` | `loop-inv` |\n\nAn `Unused filtering labels` error usually means `--claims` or `--exclude` used\nthe wrong form.\n# K syntax and operational semantics\n\nUse this reference while defining a language's configuration, grammar, and\nrewrite rules.\n\n## Configuration and cells\n\nA K configuration is the complete state rewritten by the semantics:\n\n```k\nconfiguration <T>\n                <k> $PGM:Stmt </k>\n                <state> .Map </state>\n              </T>\n```\n\nThe `<k>` cell holds the current computation. Additional cells hold only the\nstate the modeled constructs need, such as variable bindings, a heap, or output.\n\n## Grammar\n\nUser syntax is declared with `syntax` productions. Group operators of equal\nprecedence under one associativity group:\n\n```k\nsyntax AExp ::= Int | Id\n              | \"(\" AExp \")\" [bracket]\n              > left:\n                AExp \"+\" AExp [seqstrict]\n              | AExp \"-\" AExp [seqstrict]\n```\n\nAssociativity applies to the group, not independently to each production.\nPutting `[left]` on `+` and `-` separately leaves mixed input such as\n`a + b - c` ambiguous. Keep a `[bracket]` production when parentheses are part\nof the source language.\n\nConcrete program identifiers used inside claims must parse as identifiers:\n\n```k\nsyntax Id ::= \"n\" [token]\n            | \"s\" [token]\n```\n\nWithout those token productions, names such as `n` in a claim can be parsed as\nK rule variables rather than as keys in the modeled program state.\n\n## Rules and rewriting\n\nRules define one operational step:\n\n```k\nrule <k> X:Id = I:Int ; => .K ...</k>\n     <state> STATE => STATE [ X <- I ] </state>\n```\n\n- `=>` rewrites the matched content.\n- `.K` is the empty computation.\n- `...` frames the rest of a cell.\n- `strict` and `seqstrict` generate evaluation order for subterms.\n- `KResult` identifies fully evaluated values.\n\nWhen a program stops with an unmatched term at the front of `<k>`, the\nsemantics is missing a rule for that term or a required side condition did not\nhold. Treat the residual term as a diagnostic; do not add unrelated rules.\n// reference/src/semantics/subscript.k — MPY-SUBSCRIPT\n\nmodule MPY-SUBSCRIPT\n  imports MPY-CORE\n\n  // ==== positional access + negative-index normalization (used only here) ===\n  // valSeqAt is [total]: in-bounds vCons access reduces as usual; on an OPAQUE sequence (e.g.\n  // a trusted sort's sortVS(VS)) or OOB it stays an abstract total value — so indexing the\n  // opaque sorted list is DEFINED (no undischarged #Ceil), matching the old semantics' total\n  // atK. K trusts the [total] annotation; valid programs index in-bounds.\n  syntax Val ::= valSeqAt(ValSeq, Int) [function, total]\n  rule valSeqAt(vCons(V:Val, _:ValSeq), 0)     => V\n  rule valSeqAt(vCons(_:Val, S:ValSeq), I:Int) => valSeqAt(S, I -Int 1)\n       requires I >Int 0\n\n  syntax Int ::= intSeqAt(IntSeq, Int) [function]\n  rule intSeqAt(iCons(C:Int, _:IntSeq), 0)     => C\n  rule intSeqAt(iCons(_:Int, S:IntSeq), I:Int) => intSeqAt(S, I -Int 1)\n       requires I >Int 0\n\n  syntax Int ::= normIdx(Int, Int) [function, total]\n  rule normIdx(I:Int, LEN:Int) => I +Int LEN requires I  <Int 0\n  rule normIdx(I:Int, _:Int)   => I          requires I >=Int 0\n\n  // ==== Subscript: indexing obj[i] (list / tuple / str) =====================\n  // contexts (not strict attrs): the Index slot's Slice alternative must never heat\n  context Subscript(HOLE, _)\n  context Subscript(_:Val, HOLE:Expr)\n\n  // heap-object deref (covers both the index and slice forms via the Index slot)\n  rule <k> Subscript(ref(H:Int), IX:Index) => Subscript(V, IX) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  rule <k> Subscript(OBJ:Val, I:Int) => applyIndex(OBJ, I) ... </k>\n\n  syntax Val ::= applyIndex(Val, Int) [function]\n  rule applyIndex(list(VS:ValSeq),  I:Int) => valSeqAt(VS, normIdx(I, vsLen(VS)))\n  rule applyIndex(tuple(VS:ValSeq), I:Int) => valSeqAt(VS, normIdx(I, vsLen(VS)))\n  rule applyIndex(str(IS:IntSeq),   I:Int)\n    => str(iCons(intSeqAt(IS, normIdx(I, isLen(IS))), .IntSeq))\n\n  // ==== Slice: obj[lo:hi:step] (list / str) — CPython slice.indices ==========\n  syntax KItem ::= #evalB(Bound) | \"#toSome\"\n                 | #slLo(Val, Bound, Bound)\n                 | #slHi(Val, OptInt, Bound)\n                 | #slStep(Val, OptInt, OptInt)\n\n  syntax OptInt ::= \"noB\" | someB(Int)\n  rule <k> #evalB(NoBound)  => noB ... </k>\n  rule <k> #evalB(E:Expr)   => E ~> #toSome ... </k>\n  rule <k> I:Int ~> #toSome => someB(I) ... </k>\n\n  rule <k> Subscript(OBJ:Val, Slice(LO:Bound, HI:Bound, ST:Bound)) => #evalB(LO) ~> #slLo(OBJ, HI, ST) ... </k>\n  rule <k> LO:OptInt ~> #slLo(OBJ:Val, HI:Bound, ST:Bound)   => #evalB(HI) ~> #slHi(OBJ, LO, ST) ... </k>\n  rule <k> HI:OptInt ~> #slHi(OBJ:Val, LO:OptInt, ST:Bound)  => #evalB(ST) ~> #slStep(OBJ, LO, HI) ... </k>\n  // a list slice constructs a NEW object; a str slice stays a value\n  rule <k> ST:OptInt ~> #slStep(list(VS:ValSeq), LO:OptInt, HI:OptInt)\n        => #alloc(doSlice(list(VS), LO, HI, ST)) ... </k>\n       [priority(45)]\n  rule <k> ST:OptInt ~> #slStep(OBJ:Val, LO:OptInt, HI:OptInt) => doSlice(OBJ, LO, HI, ST) ... </k>\n\n  syntax Val ::= doSlice(Val, OptInt, OptInt, OptInt) [function]\n  rule doSlice(list(VS:ValSeq), LO:OptInt, HI:OptInt, ST:OptInt)\n    => list(buildVS(VS, slStart(LO, ST, vsLen(VS)), slStop(HI, ST, vsLen(VS)), slStep(ST)))\n  rule doSlice(tuple(VS:ValSeq), LO:OptInt, HI:OptInt, ST:OptInt)\n    => tuple(buildVS(VS, slStart(LO, ST, vsLen(VS)), slStop(HI, ST, vsLen(VS)), slStep(ST)))\n  rule doSlice(str(IS:IntSeq), LO:OptInt, HI:OptInt, ST:OptInt)\n    => str(buildIS(IS, slStart(LO, ST, isLen(IS)), slStop(HI, ST, isLen(IS)), slStep(ST)))\n\n  // ==== slice.indices: step / start / stop / clamp ==========================\n  syntax Int ::= slStep(OptInt) [function, total]\n  rule slStep(noB)          => 1\n  rule slStep(someB(S:Int)) => S\n\n  syntax Int ::= slStart(OptInt, OptInt, Int) [function]\n  rule slStart(noB,          ST:OptInt, _LEN:Int) => 0\n       requires slStep(ST) >Int 0\n  rule slStart(noB,          ST:OptInt, LEN:Int)  => LEN -Int 1\n       requires slStep(ST) <Int 0\n  rule slStart(someB(I:Int), ST:OptInt, LEN:Int)  => slAdjust(I, LEN, slStep(ST))\n\n  syntax Int ::= slStop(OptInt, OptInt, Int) [function]\n  rule slStop(noB,          ST:OptInt, LEN:Int)  => LEN\n       requires slStep(ST) >Int 0\n  rule slStop(noB,          ST:OptInt, _LEN:Int) => -1\n       requires slStep(ST) <Int 0\n  rule slStop(someB(I:Int), ST:OptInt, LEN:Int)  => slAdjust(I, LEN, slStep(ST))\n\n  syntax Int ::= slAdjust(Int, Int, Int) [function, total]\n  rule slAdjust(I:Int, LEN:Int, STEP:Int) => clampLo(I +Int LEN, STEP)\n       requires I  <Int 0\n  rule slAdjust(I:Int, LEN:Int, STEP:Int) => clampHi(I, LEN, STEP)\n       requires I >=Int 0\n\n  syntax Int ::= clampLo(Int, Int) [function, total]\n  rule clampLo(J:Int, _STEP:Int) => J\n       requires J >=Int 0\n  rule clampLo(J:Int, STEP:Int)  => #if STEP <Int 0 #then -1 #else 0 #fi\n       requires J <Int 0\n\n  syntax Int ::= clampHi(Int, Int, Int) [function, total]\n  rule clampHi(I:Int, LEN:Int, _STEP:Int) => I\n       requires I  <Int LEN\n  rule clampHi(I:Int, LEN:Int, STEP:Int)  => #if STEP <Int 0 #then LEN -Int 1 #else LEN #fi\n       requires I >=Int LEN\n\n  // ==== build the strided sub-sequence (indices in range by construction) ====\n  syntax ValSeq ::= buildVS(ValSeq, Int, Int, Int) [function]\n  rule buildVS(VS:ValSeq, I:Int, STOP:Int, STEP:Int)\n    => vCons(valSeqAt(VS, I), buildVS(VS, I +Int STEP, STOP, STEP))\n       requires (STEP >Int 0 andBool I <Int STOP) orBool (STEP <Int 0 andBool I >Int STOP)\n  rule buildVS(_:ValSeq, I:Int, STOP:Int, STEP:Int) => .ValSeq\n       requires notBool ((STEP >Int 0 andBool I <Int STOP) orBool (STEP <Int 0 andBool I >Int STOP))\n\n  syntax IntSeq ::= buildIS(IntSeq, Int, Int, Int) [function]\n  rule buildIS(IS:IntSeq, I:Int, STOP:Int, STEP:Int)\n    => iCons(intSeqAt(IS, I), buildIS(IS, I +Int STEP, STOP, STEP))\n       requires (STEP >Int 0 andBool I <Int STOP) orBool (STEP <Int 0 andBool I >Int STOP)\n  rule buildIS(_:IntSeq, I:Int, STOP:Int, STEP:Int) => .IntSeq\n       requires notBool ((STEP >Int 0 andBool I <Int STOP) orBool (STEP <Int 0 andBool I >Int STOP))\nendmodule\n// reference/src/semantics/int.k — MPY-INT\n// Int cases of the operator dispatch; Python // and % are floored (pyMod).\n\nmodule MPY-INT\n  imports MPY-CORE\n\n  rule applyUn(\"-\", I:Int) => 0 -Int I\n\n  rule applyBin(\"+\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 +Int I2\n  // Bool participates in int arithmetic (x += (a == b))\n  rule applyBin(\"+\",  I:Int, B:Bool) => I +Int #if B #then 1 #else 0 #fi\n  rule applyBin(\"+\",  B:Bool, I:Int) => #if B #then 1 #else 0 #fi +Int I\n  rule applyBin(\"-\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 -Int I2\n  rule applyBin(\"*\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 *Int I2\n  rule applyBin(\"%\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => pyMod(I1, I2)\n  rule applyBin(\"//\", I1:Int, I2:Int) => (I1 -Int pyMod(I1, I2)) /Int I2\n  rule applyBin(\"**\", I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 ^Int I2 requires I2 >=Int 0\n\n  syntax Int ::= pyMod(Int, Int) [function]\n  rule pyMod(I1:Int, I2:Int) => ((I1 %Int I2) +Int I2) %Int I2\n\n  rule applyCmp(\"<\",  I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 <Int  I2\n  rule applyCmp(\"<=\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 <=Int I2\n  rule applyCmp(\">\",  I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 >Int  I2\n  rule applyCmp(\">=\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 >=Int I2\n  rule applyCmp(\"==\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 ==Int I2\n  rule applyCmp(\"!=\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 =/=Int I2\nendmodule\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fad56-2d3e-7530-a30d-8104258d3287"}}}
